To prevent browser trackpad back/forward swipe navigation, you should primarily use the CSS `overscroll-behavior` property[1][2][3]. While JavaScript-only solutions have been used historically, they are often less performant or inconsistent across modern browsers[4][5].

### 1. Recommended Solution: CSS
The most effective and modern way to disable overscroll history navigation is by setting the `overscroll-behavior-x` property on the elements that are causing the issue (or globally on the `body`)[2][3].

```css
/* Apply to the specific container that scrolls horizontally */
.your-scrollable-container {
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
}

/* Or apply globally if needed */
body {
  overscroll-behavior-x: none;
}
```

*   **`contain`**: Prevents scroll chaining and prevents the browser's default navigation gesture when the element reaches its scroll boundary[6][3][5].
*   **`none`**: Similar to `contain`, but also prevents other default browser behaviors[7].
*   **Browser Support**: This is supported in most modern desktop browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge)[3]. Note that Safari has historically had inconsistent support for this property regarding navigation gestures[2][5].

---

